# Retichat web client — cache policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# ====================
# This app has no build step: index.html declares an importmap and the browser
# loads ~34 ES modules straight off the static host. Staleness was managed by
# hand, with a `?v=` query string appended to individual import specifiers.
#
# That does not work, and on 2026-08-17 the tree proved it: 8 of 34 local
# imports carried a `?v=` tag and 26 did not. `link.js`, `packet.js`,
# `resource.js`, `post_interface.js` and `lxmf_router.js` — every module the
# transport actually lives in — were fetched at a bare URL, and retichat.com
# sent no Cache-Control header at all. With only Last-Modified to go on, a
# browser applies its own heuristic freshness (commonly 10% of the document's
# age), so a fix committed, deployed and hash-verified could still not be
# running in the tab in front of you. A fix "regressing" with no code change is
# indistinguishable from a fix that was reverted, and far harder to chase.
#
# The commit "chore: version the whole changed module chain, not just the entry
# point" is what maintaining this by hand looks like: correct that day, wrong
# again the next time a module changes and the chain is not walked.
#
# So: no hand-maintained version strings anywhere in the source, and the server
# says plainly that these files must be revalidated. `no-cache` still lets the
# browser store them — it just may not reuse them without asking, so the normal
# case is a 304 with no body, not a re-download.
#
# verify-deploy.sh asserts this header is live. If mod_headers were ever
# unavailable, or this file failed to upload, the deploy fails loudly instead of
# quietly returning to heuristic caching.

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  <FilesMatch "\.(?:js|mjs|css|html|json)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# The icon is content-stable; let it cache normally.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  <FilesMatch "\.(?:png|ico)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=86400"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# ES modules must be served with a JavaScript MIME type or the browser refuses
# them outright. cPanel's default AddType covers .js; .mjs is not always mapped.
<IfModule mod_mime.c>
  AddType application/javascript .mjs
</IfModule>
